  "dom_id": "step-q85235",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224989/assets/fd812d1d33801a3ce8368feca0e7d6b87e02bcfab5d904d9b59172d95d0204e5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224989/assets/e087efb9eb18158005722dd6628cc8f19b22a2b33cad8f67205b8ea398e62c54.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$",
